🔍 Explainability of the Model (Why X → Y)

The customer churn prediction model uses machine learning to map customer attributes (X) to a churn outcome (Y). To ensure transparency and trust, explainability techniques were applied to understand how input features influence predictions.

A Random Forest classifier was selected due to its ability to provide feature importance scores. Feature importance analysis identifies which customer attributes contribute most significantly to churn predictions.

The analysis revealed that features such as customer tenure, monthly charges, and total charges have a strong influence on the model’s decision-making process. For example, customers with shorter tenure and higher monthly charges were more likely to be classified as churn risks.

This explainability allows stakeholders to understand why a customer is predicted to churn and enables informed business decisions, such as targeted retention strategies. By incorporating explainability, the model avoids being a black box and aligns with responsible and ethical AI principles.